// std.mem.mem_alloc
//
// LAW 1: there is NO ambient allocator. Anything that needs memory
// takes an Alloc, so "does this allocate?" is answered by reading
// the signature and never by reading the body. No Alloc parameter,
// no allocation.
//
// Alloc is an INTERFACE, which in Zen is an ordinary struct whose
// fields happen to be functions — nothing marks it special because
// nothing needs to. So an Alloc VALUE is the fat value every trait
// value is: a receiver pointer plus one function pointer per
// method, built on the stack, copied by value, never boxed and
// never allocated. The law is satisfied rather than side-stepped.

Ptr = std.mem.mem_ptr

// One variant, so the bar LEADS. Without it `AllocError =
// OutOfMemory` and `Alias = Shape` are the same three tokens, and a
// parser would need a guess where it now needs none.
//
// There is exactly one way to run out of memory, and no allocator
// invents a second reason: law 4 says a reason is never invented.
AllocError* = | OutOfMemory

// The allocator interface. Everything that allocates takes one.
// The implementations are an arena (env.mem.alloc(), and the one
// this library ships), a fixed buffer, and C's malloc.
//
// EVERY METHOD HERE TAKES `self: @Self`, NEVER `self :: @Self`.
// This is the load-bearing line in the module and the whole reason
// the file is worth reading twice:
//
//   an Alloc is a HANDLE. It is two words, it copies freely, and it
//   is NOT Drop. The Arena behind it owns the memory and is what
//   Drop applies to. Allocating changes the arena behind the
//   handle, not the handle's own bytes — so a bitwise copy of the
//   receiver sees the change, and DESIGN.md's test says the method
//   is `:`.
//
// What it buys: Vec stores `alloc: Alloc` as an IMMUTABLE field and
// Vec.grow calls realloc through it. Written the other way, that
// call is illegal, every collection in the library needs a mutable
// allocator field, and the shallowness of `::` buys nothing at all.
// Get this wrong here and it is wrong in every file above.
Alloc* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): an impl must provide these two.
    // Everything else in std is built out of them.
    //
    // raw is bytes and nothing more — size and alignment in, an
    // address out. Naming what the bytes are is `Ptr.to`, one
    // level down
    raw* = (self: @Self, size: usize, align: usize) Res<Ptr<u8>, AllocError>

    // `count` is the NEW element count. A null p is a fresh
    // allocation, which is what lets an empty Vec — one that owns
    // no pages — grow through the same call as a full one
    realloc* = <T>(self: @Self, p: Ptr<T>, count: usize) Res<Ptr<T>, AllocError>

    // optional hook (`::=`, no body). An arena no-ops this: it
    // frees every page at once when it drops, which is the trade
    // an arena exists to make. A malloc allocator does the real
    // thing. Neither is a stub
    free* ::= <T>(self: @Self, p: Ptr<T>) ()

    // the typed conveniences: one T's worth of storage, already
    // named, so a caller never writes a size or an alignment down
    create* ::= <T>(self: @Self) Res<Ptr<T>, AllocError>

    // Vec and Map return a BARE value, not a Res: an empty one owns
    // no pages yet, so construction cannot fail. The first add
    // allocates, and THAT is what returns Res. The signature is
    // the documentation — a call with no Res is a call that did not
    // touch memory
    Vec* ::= <T>(self: @Self) Vec<T>
    Map* ::= <K, V>(self: @Self) Map<K, V>

    // String is the exception, and it is an honest one: it takes a
    // format and must hold the formatted result, so it allocates at
    // once and says so. The empty overload is the buffer
    // Display.toString fills — `sb ::= a.String().try()` — and
    // resolution picks by arity, the ordinary rule
    String* ::= (self: @Self, fmt: str, args: ...) Res<String, AllocError>
    String* ::= (self: @Self) Res<String, AllocError>
}
